# Iris VPU. Both rows follow the same upstream convention: WHENCE declares the requested name as
# a SYMLINK to a signed variant, and cgit's plain/ endpoint cannot serve a symlink (it 404s), so
# the real blob is pulled and RENAMED on install. Which variant is not guessable from the name —
# take it from WHENCE, e.g. `Link: qcom/vpu/vpu30_p4.mbn -> vpu30_p4_s7.mbn`. Getting it wrong
# means a signature the SoC rejects, not a missing file.
#
# gen33 (SM8650): iris_platform_vpu3x.c sm8650_data -> "qcom/vpu/vpu33_p4.mbn"
qcom/vpu/vpu33_p4_s7.mbn   qcom/vpu/vpu33_p4.mbn    7b829fc1c8ce7cca836d10e898b99c5bcbd86e22073b690147168c9d0a5de378
# gen30 (SM8550 / QCS8550): iris_platform_vpu3x.c sm8550_data -> "qcom/vpu/vpu30_p4.mbn".
# Added 2026-08-18. Every qcs8550 board already had `&iris { status = "okay"; }` (via
# qcs8550-ayn-common.dtsi / qcs8550-ayaneo-pocket-common.dtsi) and CONFIG_VIDEO_QCOM_IRIS=m
# comes from defconfig, so the node probed and then failed the firmware request — hardware
# video decode was simply absent on the whole SM8550 half of the fleet. Do NOT reach for
# vpu30_p4_s6.mbn: that is a different signing, requested by a different platform desc
# (iris_vpu30_p4_s6_gen2_desc), and it is not what sm8550_data asks for.
qcom/vpu/vpu30_p4_s7.mbn   qcom/vpu/vpu30_p4.mbn    98d40a86af3b300bb55faa694bf130f4d4de8d4b5c5ba74e03214f5037368ae0
# gen35 (SM8750): iris sm8750_data -> "qcom/vpu/vpu35_p4.mbn"; WHENCE: Link:
# qcom/vpu/vpu35_p4.mbn -> vpu35_p4_s7.mbn. Both SM8750 boards enable &iris (the AYN
# common DTSI carries the node), same one-decoder situation as the SoCs above.
qcom/vpu/vpu35_p4_s7.mbn   qcom/vpu/vpu35_p4.mbn    26c92bd0e62a131e487431cf63a1b50fee83d8f7094f04df4dfbc523881ce0fc

